@startuml training-large-corpora-memory
' Every place a large corpus can blow up the heap, and the mechanism that bounds
' it. Read top to bottom as the data path: each red box is an unbounded
' accumulation, each teal box is the bound that contains it. The three-way split
' at the bottom is the actual decision an operator makes - in-memory training,
' WAL-accumulator training, or the sharded Google Books importer.
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/training-large-corpora-memory.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 1 in docs/training/large-corpora.md §2.
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam rectangle {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  roundCorner 12
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #ECEFF1
  BorderColor #546E7A
  FontColor #263238
}
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
title Large-corpus training — the four memory pressures and their bounds

legend top right
  |= Colour |= Meaning |
  |<#C62828>     | Unbounded accumulation (the hazard) |
  |<#00838F>     | The mechanism that bounds it |
  |<#1565C0>     | Resulting training mode |
endlegend

rectangle "**Corpus on disk**\ntens of GiB" as disk #455A64

rectangle "**① Corpus resident in RAM**\nnaively: read it all, then train" as p1 #C62828
rectangle "**PrefetchingReader**\nbounded batch queue,\nram_fraction = 0.10;\nreaders stream, never materialise" as b1 #00838F

rectangle "**② N-gram table**\n≈ order × tokens entries\nbefore any pruning" as p2 #C62828
rectangle "**NgramAccumulator**\nWAL-backed, on disk\n(""--checkpoint <DIR>"")" as b2 #00838F

rectangle "**③ Continuation-count pass**\nHashMap⟨String, HashSet⟨String⟩⟩;\n2–5 GiB beyond ≈ 5·10⁶ n-grams" as p3 #C62828
rectangle "**Import pre-computed statistics**\nthe Google Books path derives the MKN\ncontinuation/frequency tables during merge" as b3 #00838F

rectangle "**④ Importer overlay**\nper-shard lock-free write buffer" as p4 #C62828
rectangle "**Three nested bounds**\n""--tx-chunk-size"" — per transaction\n""--lockfree-flush-threshold"" — per shard\n""--overlay-budget-gib"" — resident heap" as b4 #00838F

rectangle "**In-memory training**\n""TrainerBuilder::train""\ncorpora ≲ 1 GiB" as r1 #1565C0
rectangle "**Checkpointed training**\n""train ngram --checkpoint""\ninterruptible · resumable" as r2 #1565C0
rectangle "**Sharded import**\n""train import-google-books""\n10⁹ and more n-grams" as r3 #1565C0

disk --> p1
p1 --> b1 : bounded by
b1 --> p2
p2 --> b2 : bounded by
b2 --> p3
p3 --> b3 : bounded by
b3 --> p4
p4 --> b4 : bounded by

b4 --> r1
b4 --> r2
b4 --> r3

note right of p3
  This is the pass that most often kills a
  from-scratch build on a big corpus: a **second,
  in-memory pass over every n-gram**, and it is
  not incremental. It is the reason the Google
  Books path computes these statistics during
  the merge instead.
end note

@enduml
